      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1075326017,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> Moving to Ubuntu 22.04 generated spurious ABI compliance failures. Try to fix that. Works for me locally.\n\nI tried doing a build on my Ubuntu 22.04 VM.  CMake was run with\n\n```\nCFLAGS=\"-Og -gdwarf-4\" cmake -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=None --BUILD_wireshark=OFF\n```\n\nThe compiler is\n\n```\ncc (Ubuntu 11.2.0-19ubuntu1) 11.2.0\nCopyright (C) 2021 Free Software Foundation, Inc.\nThis is free software; see the source for copying conditions.  There is NO\nwarranty; not even for MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.\n```\n\nwith:\n\n```\nubu22-04$ abi-dumper --version\nABI Dumper 1.2\nCopyright (C) 2019 Andrey Ponomarenko's ABI Laboratory\nLicense: GNU LGPL 2.1 <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>\nThis program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify it.\n\nWritten by Andrey Ponomarenko.\nubu22-04$ abi-compliance-checker --version\nABI Compliance Checker (ABICC) 2.3\nCopyright (C) 2018 Andrey Ponomarenko's ABI Laboratory\nLicense: GNU LGPL 2.1 <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>\nThis program is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify it.\n\nWritten by Andrey Ponomarenko.\n```\n\nand the results for abi-compliance-checker had the same pile of warnings for libwireshark that I saw from the GitLab builders.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
